From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 15:34:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: an attention/expert split for GLM-5.3-Flash
 over MCDMA (--experts remote)

One GPU serves everything but the routed experts (`tensorfold serve ... --experts remote`); two expert nodes
(`tensorfold experts MODEL --rank 0|1 --master ATTENTION`) each hold one TP2 half of every MoE layer's routed experts.
Per MoE layer the attention node sends its normed rows, picks and weights to both expert nodes and gets two fp32
partials back while its own shared expert runs; hc_post sums the three.

- The wire is MCDMA's link daemons (ashhart/MCDMA): per MoE layer one packed request into each expert's mailbox and
  one reply back. GPU-driven by default where the device's probe passes (cuStreamWriteValue64/cuStreamWaitValue64 on
  the serving stream, no host sync per layer), CPU-driven otherwise; TF_AFD_MCDMA_MODE=auto|gpu|cpu.
- afd.AfdWire holds the engine hooks (begin, exchange, collect) over an afd_transport.ExpertTransport seam: the
  TCPStore rendezvous, the settings handshake, the heartbeat watchdog, each forward's seq/rows/mode, and the opt-in
  wire check (TF_AFD_CHECK) and exchange timer (TF_AFD_TIME).
- Every window runs eager over the wire: graphs.py captures nothing on the attention node. The expert node refuses
  any forward mode but eager and prompt chunk.
- The attention node loads world-1 weights without the routed experts (EXL3 checkpoints; anything else is refused at
  load) and plans its memory without the experts' scratch. The expert node loads only its half.
- tools/mcdma_streamops_probe.py checks that a GPU can drive the exchange from its stream, before the first boot.

Tests: tests/test_glm_afd_transport.py, test_glm_afd_mcdma.py and test_glm_afd_mcdma_cuda.py on the CPU (a fake
MCDMA daemon pair and CUDA driver, the attention's real AfdWire); tests/cuda/test_glm_afd.py on the GPU.

TensorFold is Apache-2.0 (since v0.6.0); these additions are offered under the same license. MCDMA is used at
runtime through its daemons and libmcdma-rpc.so; none of its code is copied.

Credits: Hugh Madden (@dangerm00se, github.com/hughmadden) wrote glm53f-afd, the engine that serves GLM-5.3-Flash
with attention on one RTX 5090 and the routed experts on DGX Sparks over RDMA, and mimo26f-afd, its serving shell,
wire and RDMA transport. T.J. Purtell (tpurtell, @wrldsuksgo2mars) wrote ds41rt, glmrt and cuteafd, engines that
run attention on RTX GPUs and the routed experts on DGX Sparks. This commit uses no code from those engines. MCDMA
and TensorFold are by Ash Hart (@ashhart).

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (planning) and GLM-5.3-Flash (execution), via Hermes

